# Error Handling

## Repository contract

Repositories return `Flow<RequestResult<T>>` (see `mobile-data-layer.mdc`). They never throw to the caller.

```kotlin
override fun getGuides(): Flow<RequestResult<List<Guide>>> = flow {
    emit(RequestResult.InProgress)
    runCatching { apolloClient.query(...).execute().dataOrThrow() }
        .onSuccess { emit(RequestResult.Success(it.toDomain())) }
        .onFailure { emit(RequestResult.Error(it)) }
}
```

## runCatching discipline

`runCatching` swallows `CancellationException`, which breaks structured concurrency in suspend contexts. Two rules:

1. **Never wrap a suspend block with `runCatching` and silently emit an error.** If you must, rethrow `CancellationException`:
   ```kotlin
   try { ... } catch (ce: CancellationException) {
       throw ce
   } catch (t: Throwable) {
       emit(RequestResult.Error(t))
   }
   ```
2. **A `coRunCatching` helper is planned** (`common/extensions/CoroutineExtensions.kt`). Once added, replace all current `runCatching` usages in suspend / `flow {}` blocks. Track this in `mobile-roadmap.mdc`.

## UseCase layer

UseCases pass through `RequestResult` and map success payloads. They do not catch — let the repository own error surfacing.

```kotlin
fun invoke(): Flow<RequestResult<List<GuideUI>>> = repository.getGuides()
    .map { result ->
        when (result) {
            is RequestResult.Success -> RequestResult.Success(result.data.map { it.toGuideUI() })
            is RequestResult.Error -> result
            RequestResult.InProgress -> result
        }
    }
```

## ViewModel layer

ViewModels reduce `RequestResult` into `ViewState`:

```kotlin
useCase.invoke().collect { result ->
    _viewStates.value = when (result) {
        RequestResult.InProgress -> GuidesViewState.Loading
        is RequestResult.Error -> GuidesViewState.Error
        is RequestResult.Success -> GuidesViewState.Display(...)
    }
}
```

ViewModels never log errors — logging is a repository responsibility (and currently absent, see roadmap).

## Logging

There is no logging library wired up yet. `println()` was removed in commit `64ce5e9` and must not be reintroduced. Add Napier (or similar) when introducing crash reporting; until then, errors surface only through `RequestResult.Error`.
